  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Thursday)\nIssued at 101 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe rest of today will be sunny and warm with afternoon temps in \nthe 80s to low 90s. (Highs like this are 20 to 30 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year) Winds will be gusty out of the \nsouthwest to south at around 10 to 20 mph with gusts near 30 to 35\nmph.\n\nSaturday will continue the sunny skies and warm, well above \nnormal high temps. Expected highs are in the 80s to low 90s again \nwith gusty southwest winds. Elevated fire weather conditions are \nlikely due to low RH and gusty winds, more information can be \nfound in the FIRE WEATHER section below.\n\nAfter Saturday, a change in the weather pattern will happen. \nUpper level winds will swing to the west/southwest as a trough of \nlow pressure deepens over the Pacific northwest. And that low is \nprojected to track southward along the coast before kicking \neastward toward the the central US. That pattern change will bring\nmuch cooler weather to the area, high temps will drop to the 50s \nand 60s and low temps will drop to the 30s and 40s.\n\nThis change will also bring back the rain chances. The first \nround will begin to move into the far western CWA zones late \nSaturday with PoPS lingering into Monday night for the entire CWA.\n\nBeyond Monday, forecast confidence diminishes. The previously \nmentioned trend is the same in the weather model projections; an \nupper level low deepens southward along the west coast while an \nupper level high pressure builds along the southern coast, then \naround mid-week, the low will track eastward through the central \nstates. But the details in this story are what differs. The GEFS \nhas a stronger low, when compared to the EURO. And the EURO ENS \nhas a stronger high pressure, when compared to the GEFS. These \ndifferences mean differences in timing and strength of each round \nof rain as they pass through the area. There are even some \nscenarios that keep the rain south of the CWA, leaving us dry. So \nstay plugged into the forecast throughout the week.",
